ecent research was  from BCG and they tried to understand  what are the elements of diversity that  improve revenue and they talked about  specifically innovation revenue so  inventions creations they actually  inspected six different elements and  they actually found that four of them so  surprisingly not age and not to  education has an impact on innovation  but gender the country of origin the  industry where people come from and the  types of career paths that people have  been through mean that you have  different creative solving problem  Roach's that you can actually tap into  that help you innovate now anyone in a  leadership role will know time  management is really hard and so this is  where actually you have to get really  good at managing your time one of my  favorite sort of tools is this quadrant  and it helps you really understand where  you should spend time so for instance  there's things that are less important  and less urgent all those slack  notifications checking them every five  minutes Twitter every notification you  get on Twitter do you need to go log in  these are things that are not so  important not so urgent you should sort  of stop doing that so I know some people  that say I'll only check email twice a  day or I'll only check slap twice a day  between these hours if it's really  urgent I'm sure you can come and find me  alright so if it is really urgent there  are ways to get in contact find your  flow and that's really important for  things that are less important but it  urgent this is where you can actually  delegate it to people right so if people  get it wrong then it probably isn't  gonna have a big impact if it's wrong  the first time and it gives people an  opportunity to grow and contribute to  something very important and obviously  the things that are important and urgent  those production outages incidents you  need to act on it immediately but the  interesting quadrant that often gets  left out is this thing that is important  but less urgent and these are the things  that nobody asks you to do right these  are the things that you're responsible  for but nobody is actively asking you  for a plan of what is your technical  bird one how are you dealing with your  architecture as it revolves these are  things that you have to work out what is  important and where you find time to  discuss this with the team and this is  where you need to decide to make time in  your calendar to make sure you think  about this so in my calendar I would  normally have planning blocks and as  part of those planning box I would  refuse meetings and this is exactly what  I was trying to do carve out time for  those important but less urgent things  so fine if there was something that  needed to be dealt with that was urgent  I could deal with it but I need to make  sure that I had time to think about am i  adding the most value as a leader to  help amplify the success of the team and  you need to make the time to work out  what's important for you nobody will  often tell you that this matrix actually  is called the Eisenhower matrix after  an Eisenhower and there's a lot of sort  of reading about this but the summary of  this kind of section is really thinking  about you know need to take a mental  model shift from moving from maker to  multiplier so you no longer get rewarded  for the things that you produce as an  individual it becomes less concrete you  have to appreciate the value that your  whole team creates and it's less output  driven as a result and you need to sort  of take the shifts from being a maker  and become a multiplier so we've gone on  this journey try to understand some of  the different stories what is the tech  lead spices and Ruggles and hopefully  you have some tools to make you a much  more effective tech lead some of the  keys to growth that we talked about that  I want to sort of summarize are making  sure that you recognize that as you go  on this journey that this tech lead role  is a role change it requires different  skills and strengths things you will  have accumulated experience points for  but you need to develop those experience  points it requires these leadership  skills that often organisations will not  have given you training and support for  and so you need to find ways to actually  grow them for yourself but the good  thing is that other people have been on  this journey and that there are many  other resources out there so build your  support network talk to people about how  they approach things find new ways of  adding more to your sort of toolkit and  make sure you practice some of these  things and the final lesson is really  making sure you take this mental model  shift from moving from maker to  multiplier mode so thank you very much I  think we're out of time but I'll stay  around for questions if you like I don't  wanna hold you back from lunch and if  you also want we have some sort of  laptop security covers which are very  good if you like to have privacy from  your camera thank you very much  [Applause]  yes  so the pupils I don't know if you come  across Simon Brown
 

